        StudySection(id: "big_picture", title: "The Big Picture: What You Are Actually Selling", color: Color(red: 0.20, green: 0.45, blue: 0.70), lightColor: Color(red: 0.90, green: 0.95, blue: 1.0), icon: "globe.americas.fill", objectives: [
            StudyObjective(id: "bp_obj_1", title: "Understand the four foundational pillars of Agentforce RAG and how to position them to clients.", topics: [
                StudyTopic(id: "bp_1_1", number: 1, question: "What are the four foundational pillars of Agentforce RAG and what does each one mean?", answer: "The four pillars are: (1) Grounding — the agent answers from your proprietary data, not general LLM knowledge, eliminating hallucinations about your products, policies, and processes. (2) Trust — the Einstein Trust Layer ensures data never trains third-party LLMs, sensitive PII is masked before it leaves the org, and every AI interaction is audited. (3) Intelligence Infrastructure — Data 360 (formerly Data Cloud) is the vector database, identity engine, audit store, and analytics layer powering every enterprise-grade Agentforce feature. (4) Continuous Improvement — Agentforce Observability closes the loop between production conversations, content quality, and agent improvement. It is a product, not a project."),
                StudyTopic(id: "bp_1_2", number: 2, question: "How should a Success Architect position Agentforce RAG before a client meeting?", answer: "RAG with Agentforce is not a chatbot feature. It is a governed, enterprise-grade AI delivery system. Clients who understand the four pillars — Grounding, Trust, Intelligence Infrastructure, and Continuous Improvement — make better architecture decisions. The architect's job is to ensure the client understands all four pillars before signing the SOW, because each pillar shapes deployment decisions, Data 360 requirements, and long-term success metrics."),
